::STAT_U01_L05_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart title is Favorite Weekend Activity. What does the title identify?
{
=A. The context of the data display#Correct. The title tells what the display is about.
~B. The exact value of every data point#A title gives context, not every value.
~C. The cause of the pattern#A title does not prove cause.
~D. The scale interval#Scale intervals are found on an axis, not in the title.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A vertical axis says Frequency. What does frequency mean in the display?
{
~A. Percent always#Percent is relative frequency, not frequency.
=B. Count#Correct. Frequency is the number of cases in a category or interval.
~C. The category name#Categories are labels, not counts.
~D. The graph title#Frequency is a quantity, not the title.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram bar over 20-29 has height 8. What does that mean?
{
~A. The average is 8#The bar height is frequency, not average.
~B. The interval length is 8#The interval length is determined by endpoints.
=C. Eight values fall in the interval from 20 to 29#Correct. Histogram bar height shows how many values are in that interval.
~D. There are exactly 20 values#20 is part of the interval label, not the frequency.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
In a scatterplot, what do the horizontal and vertical axes usually show?
{
~A. Two category names only#Category-only variables do not fit a standard scatterplot.
~B. One frequency table#A frequency table is not shown by two scatterplot axes.
~C. Parts of a whole#Parts of a whole are shown by pie or segmented bar displays.
=D. Two numerical variables#Correct. Scatterplot axes show paired numerical measurements.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A pie chart slice is labeled 25%. What does that quantity represent?
{
=A. One category is one-fourth of the whole#Correct. 25% means one part out of the whole group.
~B. There are exactly 25 people#A percent is not a count unless the total is known.
~C. The category has rank 25#Percent is a share, not rank.
~D. The category causes the whole#A pie chart does not show causation.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which conclusion is reasonable from a bar chart showing counts by favorite subject?
{
~A. The tallest bar caused students to choose the subject#Counts do not prove cause.
=B. The tallest bar represents the subject with the most responses#Correct. In a count bar chart, taller means more responses.
~C. The average subject is the tallest bar#Category labels cannot be averaged.
~D. Every student likes only that subject#Other bars may also have responses.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A display has no units on a numerical axis. What problem can this create?
{
~A. The graph automatically becomes categorical#Missing units do not change the data type.
~B. The graph proves no pattern exists#Labels do not determine whether a pattern exists.
=C. The quantities may be hard to interpret correctly#Correct. Units explain what the numerical values measure.
~D. All answer choices become correct#Assessment choices are unrelated to graph labels.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A relative frequency table gives values 0.20, 0.35, and 0.45. What do these values represent?
{
~A. Raw category names#The values are numerical proportions.
~B. Axis labels only#They are quantities, not labels.
~C. Exact causes of outcomes#Relative frequencies summarize data; they do not prove causes.
=D. Proportions of the total#Correct. Relative frequencies are parts of the total.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A box plot has a long right whisker. What component is being interpreted?
{
=A. Spread on the high-value side of the distribution#Correct. A longer whisker shows more spread in that direction.
~B. Category count#Box plots summarize numerical data.
~C. Part of a whole#That describes pie charts or segmented bars.
~D. Two-variable association#That describes scatterplots.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which statement avoids overclaiming from a data display?
{
~A. The display proves one variable caused the other#Most displays alone do not prove causation.
=B. The display shows an association in this data set#Correct. This describes what is shown without claiming cause.
~C. The display explains every student behavior#Displays summarize data and have limits.
~D. The display makes labels unnecessary#Labels are needed for interpretation.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart category has height 12. What additional information is needed to convert that count to a percent?
{
~A. The color of the bar#Color does not determine percent.
~B. The category name length#Name length is irrelevant.
=C. The total number of responses#Correct. Percent equals category count divided by total responses.
~D. The title font size#Font size is formatting, not data.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which display component tells what each axis measures?
{
~A. Background color#Color is formatting.
~B. Border thickness#Borders do not identify variables.
~C. Answer letter#Answer letters are not graph components.
=D. Axis label#Correct. Axis labels identify variables and units.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A graph shows bus: 30 students and car: 20 students. What quantity is 30?
{
=A. Frequency for the bus category#Correct. The number is a count for that category.
~B. Relative frequency for all categories#A count is not a relative frequency unless divided by total.
~C. A numerical interval#Bus is a category, not an interval.
~D. A causal effect#The count does not show cause.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A student says the largest pie slice means that group caused the result. What is the error?
{
~A. Pie charts cannot have large slices#Pie slices can vary in size.
=B. A largest slice shows largest share, not cause#Correct. Pie charts show proportions, not causation.
~C. Categories cannot be counted#Categories can be counted.
~D. Percentages cannot be shown visually#Pie charts can show percentages visually.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which interpretation fits a scatterplot point at (3, 80) for hours studied and test score?
{
~A. Three students scored 80 total#The point is a pair, not a count of students.
~B. 80 students studied 3 hours#The coordinates are variable values, not necessarily counts.
=C. One student studied 3 hours and scored 80#Correct. A point represents paired values for one case.
~D. The average score is 3#The x-value is hours studied, not average score.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
What does scale help determine on a graph?
{
~A. Which data caused the result#Scale does not prove cause.
~B. Whether categories are allowed#Categories can be displayed with or without numerical scale.
~C. The course standard code#Standards are separate from graph scale.
=D. How numerical distances and quantities are read#Correct. Scale controls how values are marked and interpreted.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which is the best first step when interpreting a display?
{
=A. Read the title and labels#Correct. Title and labels establish context, variables, and units.
~B. Assume the tallest mark proves cause#Height shows quantity, not cause.
~C. Ignore units to save time#Units are essential for accurate interpretation.
~D. Average the category names#Category names cannot be averaged.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A segmented bar graph compares percentages by grade. What do segment lengths represent?
{
~A. Raw counts only#Segmented bars usually compare proportions.
=B. Relative frequencies within each grade#Correct. Segment lengths show proportions for categories within each group.
~C. Median values#Median applies to numerical data.
~D. Exact individual responses#The graph summarizes groups.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which display component identifies categories in a bar chart?
{
~A. Numerical intervals#Intervals belong to histograms.
~B. Correlation coefficient#That measures strength of numerical association.
=C. Category labels#Correct. Category labels name the groups being compared.
~D. Five-number summary#That belongs to box plots.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram has bars for intervals, but no title. What is missing?
{
~A. All numerical values#The bars may still show values by interval.
~B. All category labels#Histograms use intervals, not category labels.
~C. The correct answer letter#Graphs do not include quiz answer letters.
=D. Context for what the data values represent#Correct. Without a title, the viewer may not know what is being measured.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which statement is supported by a display showing 60% prefer online review?
{
=A. A majority of the group prefers online review#Correct. 60% is more than half.
~B. Online review caused higher grades#Preference data alone does not prove grade effects.
~C. Exactly 60 students prefer online review#Percent is not count unless total is known.
~D. No one prefers other methods#If 60% prefer online, 40% prefer something else or are in other categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
In a histogram, why are the bars usually adjacent?
{
~A. The categories are unrelated labels#That describes bar charts, not histograms.
=B. The intervals represent connected ranges of numerical data#Correct. Adjacent bars show continuous or ordered numerical intervals.
~C. The bars prove causation#Bar placement does not prove cause.
~D. The graph has two variables#Histograms show one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
What should a supported conclusion include?
{
~A. A claim about causes not shown#Unsupported cause claims overreach.
~B. A guess unrelated to the labels#Conclusions should connect to the display.
=C. A statement tied to the variables and quantities shown#Correct. A good conclusion uses the display evidence and context.
~D. A replacement for the data source#A conclusion does not replace the data.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A two-way table shows counts by grade and transportation method. What kind of data is represented?
{
~A. One numerical variable#There are two categorical variables.
~B. Two numerical variables#No measured quantities are paired as variables.
~C. A numerical distribution only#The table organizes categorical counts.
=D. Two categorical variables#Correct. Grade and transportation method are categories.
}

::STAT_U01_L05_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L05_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Which question checks whether a conclusion is reasonable?
{
=A. Does the display actually show evidence for the claim?#Correct. Conclusions must be supported by the displayed data.
~B. Is the graph colorful enough?#Color does not determine statistical support.
~C. Can the title be ignored?#The title provides context.
~D. Can labels be replaced with guesses?#Labels should not be guessed or ignored.
}

